{
  name: "rv_dm"

  // Formal testplan for rv_dm in Earl Grey

  import_testplans: ["hw/dv/tools/dvsim/testplans/fpv_csr_testplan.hjson"]
  testpoints: [
    // Testbench hook-up

    {
      name: fpv_hookup
      desc: '''
            Verify that the formal testbench connects to rv_dm, and that the design's own
            assertions and the TL-UL protocol checkers on its three bus ports are proven.

            Stimulus
              - Elaborate rv_dm with the Earl Grey parameters, with the JTAG transport (dmi_jtag)
                black-boxed.
              - Leave every input free, except that the DMI carries one request at a time, held
                until it is taken, scan mode is off, and the inputs Earl Grey ties off keep their
                tied values.
              - Bind the TL-UL protocol checker (tlul_assert) to regs_tl_d and mem_tl_d as
                devices and to sba_tl_h as a host.

            Checking
              - The design elaborates and every bound module connects.
              - The TL-UL assertions on the three ports and the assertions inside rv_dm are
                proven.
              - The checkers' covers are reached on each port.
            '''
      stage: V1
      tests: ["rv_dm_fpv"]
    }

    // Debug enable

    {
      name: en_open
      desc: '''
            Verify that each side of rv_dm, the JTAG side and the bus side, opens exactly when the
            life cycle, OTP and late debug enables allow it.

            Stimulus
              - Drive lc_dft_en, lc_hw_debug_en and pinmux_hw_debug_en with On, Off and invalid
                values, changing in any cycle. lc_dft_en is On only while lc_hw_debug_en is On.
              - Drive the fuse DIS_RV_DM_LATE_DEBUG with MuBi8True, MuBi8False and invalid values,
                and write LATE_DEBUG_ENABLE with MuBi32True, MuBi32False and a value one bit away
                from MuBi32True.

            Checking
              - The bus side is open when lc_dft_en and lc_hw_debug_en are On, or when
                lc_hw_debug_en is On and the fuse is MuBi8True or LATE_DEBUG_ENABLE is MuBi32True.
              - Once it has been open for N cycles, a halt request raises debug_req, an SBA access
                reaches sba_tl_h and a debug ROM fetch (0x1_0800) gets no d_error.
              - A DMI request reaches the debug module registers only while pinmux_hw_debug_en is
                On.
              - In DEV before the late enable, the debugger reaches the debug module registers but
                cannot halt or reset the chip.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: en_closed
      desc: '''
            Verify that nothing leaves rv_dm while a side is closed, that a side closes when its
            enable is withdrawn, and that neither side can reach the other while the bus side is
            closed.

            Stimulus
              - Close the bus side in DEV and let the debugger request a halt, a reset, SBA
                accesses and commands.
              - Turn lc_hw_debug_en and pinmux_hw_debug_en Off while requests on mem_tl_d and
                sba_tl_h wait for their answers.
              - Run two copies of rv_dm that differ only in the DMI requests, and two that differ
                only in the traffic on mem_tl_d, regs_tl_d and sba_tl_h.

            Checking
              - While the bus side is closed, debug_req and ndmreset_req stay 0, nothing leaves
                sba_tl_h, and every mem_tl_d request gets d_error.
              - A side closes within N cycles of its enable going Off. An access accepted before
                that still gets one answer, and an SBA access refused while closed is never sent.
              - While the JTAG side is closed, tdo and tdo_oe stay 0.
              - With the bus sides closed, the two copies of each pair give the same outputs, so
                neither side can reach the other.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: en_late_debug
      desc: '''
            Verify that boot software can open the bus side in DEV with LATE_DEBUG_ENABLE, close
            it again, and then lock it.

            Stimulus
              - In DEV with the fuse at MuBi8False, write LATE_DEBUG_ENABLE with MuBi32True, a value
                one bit away from it and MuBi32False, before and after clearing
                LATE_DEBUG_ENABLE_REGWEN.
              - Write it with a byte, a half-word and a broken integrity code.

            Checking
              - Only MuBi32True opens the bus side, and MuBi32False closes it again within N cycles
                while REGWEN is 1.
              - Once REGWEN is 0, LATE_DEBUG_ENABLE and REGWEN keep their values until reset.
              - A byte or half-word write gets d_error and changes nothing, and a broken integrity
                code also raises fatal_fault.
              - Cover the bus side opening with haltreq and ndmreset already set.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }

    // Halt, commands and resume

    {
      name: core_halt
      desc: '''
            Verify that a halt request, and nothing else, raises debug_req, and that rv_dm reports
            the core as halted once the core says it is.

            Stimulus
              - With the bus side open, write dmcontrol with haltreq set (0x8000_0001) and then
                cleared (0x0000_0001), and with hartsel all ones (0x03ff_ffc1).
              - Write resumereq, a command or ndmreset on its own, and clear dmactive.
              - Let a free core write HALTED and read FLAGS (0x1_0400) at any time.

            Checking
              - Setting haltreq raises debug_req within N cycles, and clearing it lowers it.
              - Nothing else raises debug_req, and while dmactive is 0 debug_req and ndmreset_req
                stay 0.
              - hartsel reads back 0 and the halt still reaches hart 0, as Earl Grey has one hart.
              - After the core writes HALTED, dmstatus.allhalted is 1 and FLAGS reads 0.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: core_command
      desc: '''
            Verify that each abstract command from the debugger runs once with the right result,
            and that a refused command runs nothing.

            Stimulus
              - With the hart halted, issue Access Register commands, such as a read of s1
                (0x0022_1009) and a write of s0 with data0 = 0xcafe_0001 (0x0023_1008).
              - Issue unsupported commands, such as aarsize = 4 (0x0042_1009) and Access Memory
                (0x0200_0000), and commands while the hart runs, while one is busy and while cmderr
                is not 0.
              - Let a free core write HALTED, GOING, RESUMING and EXCEPTION at any time.

            Checking
              - A supported command sets GO in FLAGS once, GOING clears it, and the next HALTED ends
                it with busy = 0. EXCEPTION ends it with cmderr = 3.
              - What the core stores to data0 (0x1_0380) is what the debugger reads, and the other
                way round.
              - A refused command sets no GO. Unsupported options give cmderr = 2, a command for a
                running hart cmderr = 4, and a command or data access while busy cmderr = 1.
              - Writing 1s to cmderr clears it, and abstractauto all ones reads back 0x00ff_0003.
              - A stray GOING, RESUMING or second HALTED changes nothing, and a command never runs
                twice.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: core_program
      desc: '''
            Verify that the code rv_dm gives the core reads or writes only the register a command
            names, and restores every register it borrows.

            Stimulus
              - Run the code rv_dm serves for each command, and the ROM's own halt, resume and
                exception paths, through a reference interpreter written from the ISA
                specifications.
              - Read and write GPRs and CSRs, such as s1 (0x0022_1009), dpc (0x0023_07b1) and x0,
                and the registers the code borrows, a0 (0x0022_100a) and s0 (0x0022_1008).
              - Run the program buffer after a transfer (0x0026_1009) with progbuf0 =
                addi s2, s2, 4 and progbuf1 = ebreak.

            Checking
              - regno 0x0 to 0xfff names a CSR and 0x1000 to 0x101f a GPR, and only that register
                is read or written. Any other regno, such as 0x1020, fails.
              - A read of a0 or s0 returns the halted program's value, and x0 stays 0.
              - With postexec, s2 grows by 4 and nothing else changes.
              - The core is back in the debug ROM within 19 instructions, and after the dret every
                GPR holds its value from the debug entry unless a command wrote it.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: core_resume
      desc: '''
            Verify that a resume request resumes a halted core exactly once, and only while no
            halt request is set.

            Stimulus
              - With the hart halted, write resumereq (0x4000_0001), and also resumereq with
                haltreq (0xc000_0001) and resumereq while the hart runs.
              - Cover a full session from a halt request to the core's RESUMING write, and the hart
                halting on its own through ebreak.

            Checking
              - A resume request sets RESUME in FLAGS and clears allresumeack, and the core's
                RESUMING write clears RESUME and sets allrunning and allresumeack.
              - A resume request is ignored while haltreq is set and while the hart runs.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: core_port
      desc: '''
            Verify that mem_tl_d and regs_tl_d answer every request once by the TL-UL rules, and
            that no bus access changes the code the core fetches or the debug module registers.

            Stimulus
              - Drive mem_tl_d and regs_tl_d from a free host, with the life cycle gate opening and
                closing around the requests.
              - Write the ROM (0x1_0800), WHERETO (0x1_0300), the abstract command (0x1_0338), the
                program buffer (0x1_0360) and FLAGS (0x1_0400), store bytes to data0 and data1,
                and mark writes as instruction fetches.
              - In a separate run, send wrong requests, such as a_size = 3.

            Checking
              - Every request gets exactly one response, with its d_source and size and a correct
                integrity code, d_error responses included.
              - A write to the ROM, WHERETO, the abstract command, the program buffer or FLAGS
                changes nothing a later fetch returns.
              - A byte store to data0 or data1 changes exactly that byte, or gets d_error and
                changes nothing. A write marked as a fetch, a wrong request and an access to an
                unimplemented address change nothing.
              - No bus access changes the debug module registers or raises debug_req or
                ndmreset_req.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }

    // CHERIoT mode

    {
      name: chr_mode
      desc: '''
            Verify that rv_dm gives the core the debug ROM and programs of its mode, RV32I or
            CHERIoT.

            Stimulus
              - Drive cheriot_ena with MuBi4False (RV32I), MuBi4True (CHERIoT) and invalid values,
                changing around the moment debug_req rises.
              - The mode does not change while the hart is halted or a command is issued.

            Checking
              - Only MuBi4True selects CHERIoT, and every other value gives the RV32I ROM and
                programs.
              - From 3 cycles after cheriot_ena changes, every ROM fetch returns the ROM of the new
                mode, and one debug entry uses one ROM throughout.
              - In CHERIoT mode the scratch registers are saved only with CSpecialRW, so borrowed
                capabilities keep their tags.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: chr_capability
      desc: '''
            Verify that 64-bit commands move a whole capability through data0 and data1 in CHERIoT
            mode.

            Stimulus
              - In CHERIoT mode, read and write s1 with aarsize = 3 (0x0032_1009, 0x0033_1009),
                using the address 0x1000_2040 with metadata 0x1f00_c008.
              - Read and write the SCRs MTCC (0x1c), MEPCC (0x1f) and depcc (0x18), and read SCR 5,
                which Ibex does not have.
              - mtcc keeps the SR permission in debug mode, as Ibex needs.

            Checking
              - A 64-bit read leaves the address in data0 and the metadata in data1.
              - A 64-bit write puts data0 and data1 in the register with tag 0, as tags do not
                travel through data0 and data1.
              - A write of depcc changes where the core resumes, and a command for an SCR Ibex does
                not have fails with cmderr = 3.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: chr_regmap
      desc: '''
            Verify that in CHERIoT mode a command for a register CHERIoT removes is refused and
            changes nothing, and a command for a register it adds works.

            Stimulus
              - In CHERIoT mode, issue commands for x16 to x31 (such as 0x0022_1010), mtvec
                (0x0022_0305) and mepc (0x0022_0341).
              - Access mshwm (0x0bc1) and mshwmb (0x0bc2) with 8, 16 and 32 bits, and mstatus with
                64 bits (0x0032_0300).

            Checking
              - A command for x16 to x31, mtvec or mepc fails and changes no register.
              - mshwm and mshwmb are read and written with cmderr = 0.
              - A 64-bit command for a CSR from 0x20 to 0xfff is refused with cmderr = 2 and runs
                nothing.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }

    // System bus access

    {
      name: sba_request
      desc: '''
            Verify that each SBA access the debugger starts gives exactly one well-formed request
            on sba_tl_h, and that nothing else leaves it.

            Stimulus
              - Start SBA reads and writes with sbaccess 0, 1 and 2 at every byte offset, such as a
                16-bit write of 0xbeef to 0x1000_0002, with sbautoincrement, sbreadonaddr and
                sbreadondata on and off.
              - Let the device stall and answer after any delay, in RV32I and CHERIoT mode.

            Checking
              - Each access sends exactly one request, with the address from sbaddress0, the size
                from sbaccess and the write data from sbdata0, and nothing else leaves sba_tl_h.
              - a_mask marks exactly the addressed bytes, 0b1100 for the 16-bit write.
              - sbreadonaddr and sbreadondata start one read each, and sbautoincrement adds the
                access size to the address.
              - Every request has a correct integrity code and is marked as a data access.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: sba_answer
      desc: '''
            Verify that each SBA answer lands where the debug specification says, with read data
            in sbdata0 and a bus error in sberror.

            Stimulus
              - Answer SBA reads after 0, 1 and 7 cycles with any data, with d_error and with a
                broken integrity code.
              - Read sbdata0 with sbreadondata set, and clear dmactive while a read waits.

            Checking
              - Read data lands in sbdata0, and sbbusy falls within N cycles.
              - d_error sets sberror = 2, and a broken integrity code sets sberror = 7 with no
                alert, as the rv_dm interfaces page says.
              - With sbreadondata, a read of sbdata0 returns the previous data, then starts the next
                read.
              - An answer that arrives after dmactive was cleared changes nothing.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: sba_refuse
      desc: '''
            Verify that rv_dm sends nothing on sba_tl_h when it must refuse an SBA access, and
            that the debugger can clear the error and try again.

            Stimulus
              - Start accesses with sbaccess = 3 to 7, a misaligned 32-bit read of 0x1000_0002, and
                accesses while sbbusy = 1 or while sberror is set.
              - Write 1s to sberror and sbbusyerror, then start a 32-bit read.

            Checking
              - An unsupported size sets sberror = 4 and a misaligned access sberror = 3, and
                neither sends anything.
              - An access while sbbusy = 1 sets sbbusyerror, and nothing is sent while sberror or
                sbbusyerror is set.
              - sberror only ever reads 0, 2, 3, 4 or 7, and writing 1s clears it so the next access
                goes out.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }

    // NDM reset, sleep and full resets

    {
      name: ndm_request
      desc: '''
            Verify that the debugger's NDM reset request reaches pwrmgr once, after the debug
            memory accesses in flight are answered.

            Stimulus
              - Set dmcontrol.ndmreset (0x0000_0003) with the core idle and during a debug memory
                fetch, and write ndmreset = 1 and then 0 at once.
              - Model the reset as pwrmgr and rstmgr give it, with rst_lc_ni asserted later, or
                never.

            Checking
              - ndmreset_req rises within N cycles, once the core's debug memory accesses in flight
                are answered.
              - A write of 1 followed at once by 0 still requests one reset, and ndmreset_req falls
                when ndmreset is written 0.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: ndm_survive
      desc: '''
            Verify that the debug session survives an NDM reset, and that rv_dm reports the reset
            until the debugger acknowledges it.

            Stimulus
              - Before the reset, set data0 = 0x1234_5678, progbuf0, cmderr and sbaddress0, and in
                DEV LATE_DEBUG_ENABLE = MuBi32True.
              - Hold rst_lc_ni low for 1, 2 and 64 cycles with rst_ni held, also during a command,
                and read dmstatus during the reset.

            Checking
              - These values are kept, and DMI reads complete during the reset.
              - After the reset the hart is not halted, the command logic is idle, and the next
                command runs normally.
              - dmstatus.allhavereset reads 1 after the reset until the debugger writes
                ackhavereset.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: ndm_latch
      desc: '''
            Verify that a halt request held through an NDM reset halts the core at its first
            instruction, and that the enable rv_dm latches for the reset is dropped once lc_ctrl
            is ready again.

            Stimulus
              - Hold haltreq = 1 through the NDM reset, with lc_hw_debug_en and lc_init_done Off
                while rst_lc_ni is low, and lc_init_done back On after any delay, or never.
              - Turn lc_hw_debug_en Off before, during and after the reset.

            Checking
              - debug_req stays 1 from the request until the core writes HALTED, so the core halts
                before its first instruction.
              - Once lc_init_done is On again, the bus side follows the live enables.
              - If lc_init_done never comes back, lc_hw_debug_clr clears the latch and the bus side
                closes within N cycles.
              - If the reset never comes, the latched enable never keeps the bus side open.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: ndm_sleep
      desc: '''
            Verify that rv_dm comes back clean after deep sleep, and keeps the debug session
            through normal sleep.

            Stimulus
              - Release rst_ni as after deep sleep.
              - Stop clk_i for 1 to N cycles without a reset, as in normal sleep.

            Checking
              - After deep sleep, dmactive, debug_req and ndmreset_req are 0 and
                dmstatus.allhavereset is 1, so the debugger activates rv_dm again.
              - Through a clock stop, every debug module register keeps its value, so no new
                activation is needed after the wakeup.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: rst_full
      desc: '''
            Verify that every full reset leaves rv_dm clean, and that rv_dm sends nothing while
            held in reset.

            Stimulus
              - Assert both resets for 1, 2 and 64 cycles, after GO, during a command, with an SBA
                access open and in DEV after LATE_DEBUG_ENABLE = MuBi32True.
              - Release rst_ni and rst_lc_ni in different cycles.

            Checking
              - After the release, dmactive, debug_req, ndmreset_req, busy, cmderr and sberror are
                0, allhavereset is 1 and LATE_DEBUG_ENABLE is MuBi32False.
              - While rst_ni is asserted, nothing leaves sba_tl_h, mem_tl_d or regs_tl_d.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }

    // Alert

    {
      name: alt
      desc: '''
            Verify that rv_dm raises fatal_fault for a request with a broken integrity code on its
            device ports, and for nothing a debugger does.

            Stimulus
              - Send requests with a broken integrity code on mem_tl_d and regs_tl_d, with debug
                open and closed.
              - Cause SBA errors, refused accesses and failed commands, and write ALERT_TEST with
                0x8000_0001 and 0x0000_0000.
              - Drive alert_rx_i from an alert handler model, with pings and its in-band reset.

            Checking
              - A broken integrity code raises fatal_fault, held until reset.
              - SBA errors, refused accesses and failed commands raise no alert.
              - Each ALERT_TEST write of 0x8000_0001 sends one alert, and none once regwen is 0.
              - rv_dm answers pings and the in-band reset as the alert protocol says.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }

    // Registers

    {
      name: dm_regs
      desc: '''
            Verify that every DMI request gets one answer, and that the debug module registers
            read back what the debug specification says.

            Stimulus
              - Send DMI reads and writes to every address from 0x00 to 0x7f, one at a time, with
                each op, and pulse the DMI reset during a request.
              - Write all ones to the unimplemented registers, data2 to data11 and progbuf8 to
                progbuf15, and dmcontrol = 0x8000_0003, then read them back.
              - Write registers while dmactive = 0, and read dmstatus, hartinfo, abstractcs and sbcs
                with the hart halted and running.

            Checking
              - Every DMI request gets exactly one answer with resp = 0, and a DMI reset during a
                request carries it out at most once.
              - Unimplemented registers read 0 and ignore writes, and dmcontrol reads back as
                0x0000_0003.
              - While dmactive = 0 writes are ignored, and after it is set the registers read their
                reset values.
              - dmstatus reads version = 2 and authenticated = 1, hartinfo reads 0x0021_2380 and
                abstractcs reads 0x0800_0002 while idle.
              - haltsum0 bit 0 is 1 exactly while the hart is halted, and nextdm reads 0.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }

    // Security countermeasures

    {
      name: sec_cm_sba_tl_lc_gate_fsm_sparse
      desc: '''
            Verify that flipping bits in the state of the SBA gate raises fatal_fault, and that no
            SBA request leaves while the bus side is closed.

            Stimulus
              - Flip one or two bits of the state register of the TL-UL gate on sba_tl_h, in
                every state, as the rv_dm block testplan does.

            Checking
              - fatal_fault is raised, as OpenTitan's countermeasure guidelines treat an invalid
                sparse state as a fatal error.
              - No SBA request leaves rv_dm while the bus side is closed.
            '''
      stage: V2S
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: sec_cm_mem_tl_lc_gate_fsm_sparse
      desc: '''
            Verify that flipping bits in the state of the debug memory gate raises fatal_fault, and
            that every debug memory access still gets an error while the bus side is closed.

            Stimulus
              - Flip one or two bits of the state register of the TL-UL gate on mem_tl_d, in
                every state.

            Checking
              - fatal_fault is raised, as for the SBA gate.
              - Every mem_tl_d request is still answered with d_error while the bus side is
                closed.
            '''
      stage: V2S
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: sec_cm_exec_ctrl_mubi
      desc: '''
            Verify that no instruction fetch from the debug memory succeeds while its fetch enable
            holds any value other than On.

            Stimulus
              - Force the multibit instruction fetch enable of the debug memory to every value
                other than its enable value.

            Checking
              - No instruction fetch from the debug memory succeeds, for any of these values.
            '''
      stage: V2S
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: sec_cm_dm_en_ctrl_lc_gated
      desc: '''
            Verify that forcing one copy of the debug enable On while the bus side is closed opens
            only that copy's function.

            Stimulus
              - With the bus side closed, force one buffered copy of the debug enable On, one copy
                per run, for instruction fetches, debug memory accesses, SBA, the halt request
                and the reset request.

            Checking
              - Only the forced copy's function opens. The other four stay closed, so there is no
                debug_req, no ndmreset_req and no SBA request, and every debug memory access gets
                d_error, apart from the forced function.
            '''
      stage: V2S
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: sec_cm_reg_we_onehot
      desc: '''
            Verify that a fault in the write-enable check of the software registers raises
            fatal_fault.

            Stimulus
              - Force the one-hot check of the register write enables behind regs_tl_d to see
                more than one write enable, or one with no write behind it.

            Checking
              - fatal_fault is raised (rv_dm block testplan, tl_intg_err).
            '''
      stage: V2S
      tests: []
    }
  ]
}
